desktop: magpie's Windows app, a WinUI 3 shell over the page magpie web serves

desktop/ is the Windows desktop app drawn in WinUI 3, with no change to
magpie at all: it starts the magpie beside it as `magpie web --addr
127.0.0.1:<free port> --no-open` with a MAGPIE_WEB_KEY of its own, reads the
link magpie prints, and opens it. The address, the key and the link are
magpie web's own, so the app is one more client of the same page and API,
beside a browser tab and the Wails app; magpie's files stay where magpie
keeps them.

The shell follows Windows' app guidance rather than the web page's layout: a
NavigationView with its pane on the left, expanded on a wide window and
icons only on a narrow one (PaneDisplayMode Auto), grouped by Work / Models /
Insights / Extend, and Settings as the pane's last item where Windows puts
it. The tray icon is Windows' own Shell_NotifyIcon, and the quick panel is a
popup that hides when it loses the focus.

Pages drawn here so far: Agents (each agent's fields and values, the app's
own menu for a value, POST /api/set) and Providers (the list, on/off in
place, the search, the turned-off fold). Every other page is the page magpie
serves, in a WebView2, on its own ?view= — so the five languages, the click
rule and the plugin pages hold there unchanged. The pages not drawn yet take
one WebView2 away each as they are drawn.

What magpie web's host has no window for, the app answers itself without
magpie knowing: a folder picker (webHost answers "in the browser magpie
can't show a folder picker") is Windows' picker, its answer handed back to
the page's own request; the panel's height is read from the page's own
scroll height, since /api/window/fit is a no-op for a browser's magpie.

What was run: dotnet build -c Debug -p:Platform=x64 (0 errors), and on this
machine the built app: it started magpie web, read the link it printed,
signed in with the key's cookie, put up the tray icon (Shell_NotifyIcon true,
a real icon handle), showed its window on the left-pane shell, and drew the
Agents page from /api/state (this computer's Claude Code and its fields) and
the Providers page from /api/providers. A magpie the user already had running
(magpie-windows-amd64, started 16/10) was not touched: this app's magpie runs
on its own port with its own key, and its ~/.config/magpie is the one
installed magpie's, read as a browser's tab reads it.

Not done yet, and said in desktop/README.md: the other seven pages are still
the web page, and notifications, magpie:// links, open at login, the title
bar's tint, and a release that ships two programs instead of one are theirs
to come.
